## Hint 1

A `Map` from name to a running total, one pass, then sort. The interesting
decisions are all in the filtering and the tie-break, not the aggregation.

## Hint 2

```ts
const totals = new Map<string, ConstructorTotal>();
for (const node of snapshot.nodes) {
  if (!allowed.has(node.type)) continue;
  const entry = totals.get(node.name) ?? { name: node.name, count: 0, selfSize: 0 };
  entry.count += 1;
  entry.selfSize += node.selfSize;
  totals.set(node.name, entry);
}
```

Mutating the entry you just read avoids a second `set` per node, which
matters when `nodes` has three million elements.

## Hint 3

Sort with an explicit tie-break:

```ts
list.sort((a, b) => b.selfSize - a.selfSize || a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
```

Without the second clause the order of equal-sized entries depends on
insertion, which makes your report change between runs on identical input.

The decision left to you: whether `selfSize` is even the right thing to rank
by. It is not, really — retained size is — and knowing why you are using the
available approximation is part of the exercise.

